  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA broad mid and upper level trough remains over the \neastern CONUS this afternoon. The H500 closed low with the trough \nhas drifted southward from southern Quebec over northern NY and\nNew England this afternoon.  H500 temps associated with the \nupper level low are -1 to -2 STDEVs below/colder than normal \nbased on the latest NAEFS guidance. In the cyclonic flow, a \nshort-wave will rotate across the region this afternoon into \ntonight focusing scattered rain and snow showers. With the cold \ntemps aloft, the pcpn will transition to snow showers and \nflurries by this evening. With the west/northwest flow in the \nboundary layer and aloft we will see some orographic enhancement\noff the western Dacks, the southern Greens and northern \nTaconics. Some lake moisture will be tapped with the \nwest/northwest flow. Light accumulations of snow will be \npossible over the west/southwest Dacks, southern Greens, \nnorthern Taconics, the northern and eastern Catskills, and \npossibly the western Mohawk Valley based on the CAMs (3-km \nHRRR/RGEM/RRFS). With marginal temps and the boundary layer flow\nveering to the northwest accumulations are expected to be light\non the order of dusting to up to an inch or so over the higher \nterrain. Some rain/snow showers may reach the Capital \nRegion/Hudson Valley, but with the boundary layer temps above \nfreezing in the mid 30s to lower 40s we are not expecting any \nsubstantial accumulation. It will remain cloudy and breezy in \nthe moist cyclonic flow with highs only the 30s and 40s this pm,\nand lows tonight will range from the mid 20s to lower 30s. \n\nBy tomorrow, the upper low begins to shift north and east of the \nregion. Mid and upper level ridging will attempt to build in from \nthe central CONUS. The boundary layer flow shifts to the north \nto northwest. In the north to northwest flow some upslope or \nlake effect snow showers may linger in the morning for the \nwestern Dacks and the northern and eastern Catskills with little\nadditional accumulation. It will remain chilly with mostly \ncloudy and breezy conditions. The skies may become partly cloudy\nbriefly in the Hudson River Valley and south of the Capital \nRegion. Max temps will still run below normal with mainly 30s \nover the higher terrain and lower to mid 40s in the valleys, as \nwe blended the NBM with the colder MAVMOS values. Partly cloudy \nskies are expected with a gradual clearing Sun night/Mon \nmorning, as high pressure slowly builds in from the Midwest. It \nwill take some time to erode the low-level moisture. Lows were \nforecasted close to the NBM with mid 20s to lower 30s across \neastern NY and western New England. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMonday begins an extended stretch of dry weather with mid and \nupper level heights increasing across the region. At the \nsurface, an anticyclone will be ridging in from the Midwest and \nOhio Valley. Temps on Monday will run a little below normal, but\nby Tuesday with abundant sunshine and the surface ridge \nbuilding in from the south and west, expect highs for mid- \nNovember to be near to slightly above normal with 50 to 55F \nreadings in the valleys and 40s over the higher terrain. Temps \nlook to run about 10 degrees above normal for the mid to latter\nportion of the week with a fair amount of sunshine due to \nstrong subsidence with the ridge. A cold front may bring some \nshowers next weekend, but overall a nice stretch of mid to late \nNovember fair and dry weather is expected.",
